<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
  <section xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-07-hud">
    <title>Heads Up Display (HUD)</title>
    <introduction>
    <p>Our game needs a User Interface (UI) to display things like
            score, a "game over" message, and a start button.</p>
    <p>Create a new scene, click the "Other Node" button and add a <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_canvaslayer.html#class-canvaslayer">CanvasLayer</url> node named
            <c>HUD</c>. "HUD" stands for "heads-up display", an informational display that
            appears as an overlay on top of the game view.</p>
    <p>The <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_canvaslayer.html#class-canvaslayer">CanvasLayer</url> node lets us draw our UI elements on
            a layer above the rest of the game, so that the information it displays isn't
            covered up by any game elements like the player or mobs.</p>
    <p>The HUD needs to display the following information:</p>
    <p>
      <ul>
      <li>Score, changed by collecting prizes.</li>
      <li>Level, changed by leveling up.</li>
      <li>A message, such as "Game Over", "Get Ready!", "Level 1"</li>
      <li>A "Start" button to begin the game.</li>
      <li>The fuel available for the rocket.</li>
       <li>The health of the ship.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>The basic node for UI elements is <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_control.html#class-control">Control</url>. To create our
            UI, we'll use two types of <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_control.html#class-control">Control</url> nodes: <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_label.html#class-label">Label</url> and <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_button.html#class-button">Button</url>.</p>
    <p>
        We also will use three types of <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_container.html#description">Container</url> to organize our Control elements. These are
        <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_margincontainer.html#class-margincontainer">MarginContainer</url>,
        <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_hboxcontainer.html#class-hboxcontainer">HBoxContainer</url>,
        and <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_vboxcontainer.html#class-vboxcontainer">VBoxContainer</url>.
    </p>
            <p>Create the following as children of the <c>HUD</c> node:</p>
    <p>
      <ul>
      <li>
        <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_margincontainer.html#margincontainer">MarginContainer</url> named <c>TopBar</c>.</li>
      <li>
        <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_vboxcontainer.html#vboxcontainer">VBoxContainer</url> named <c>Overlay</c>.</li>
      <li>
        <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_timer.html#class-timer">Timer</url> named <c>MessageTimer</c>.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>Create the following as children of the <c>Overlay</c> node:</p>
    <p>
      <ul>
      <li>
        <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_label.html#class-label">Label</url> named <c>Message</c>.</li>
      <li>
        <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_button.html#class-button">Button</url> named <c>StartButton</c>.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p> The TopBar Node should have one child that is an
        <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_hboxcontainer.html#hboxcontainer">HBoxContainer</url>
    </p>

    <p>Create the following as children of the <c>HBoxContainer</c> node:</p>
    <p>
      <ul>
      <li>
        <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_label.html#class-label">Label</url> named <c>ScoreLabel</c>.</li>
      <li>
        <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_textureprogressbar.html">TextureProgressBar</url> named <c>FuelGauge</c>.</li>
      <li>
        Do not make the Health bar yet.</li>
      </ul>
      
    </p>

    <p>Click on the <c>ScoreLabel</c> and type a number into the <c>Text</c> field in the
            Inspector. The default font for <c>Control</c> nodes is small and doesn't scale
            well. There is a font file in the<url href="https://wcu-cs-cooperlab.github.io/Fall2026GameDev/book-assets/space-farm/fonts.zip">fonts</url>
         zip file. Download, unzip the file, and add the fonts directory to your project.
            There you will find "AutumnGifts-YVKv.ttf". To use this font, do the following:</p>
    <p>Under "Theme Overrides &gt; Fonts", choose "Load" and select the "AutumnGifts-YVKv.ttf" file in fonts.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Load Custom Font Button</caption>
      <image source="custom_font_load_font.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>In the Font option of Fonts, the Load option is surrounded by a yellow box in the Theme overrides of Control inspector</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
    <p>The font size is still too small, increase it to <c>40</c> under "Theme Overrides &gt; Font Sizes".
            Once you've done this with the <c>ScoreLabel</c>, repeat the changes for the <c>Message</c> Node using font size <c>60</c> and <c>StartButton</c> node using font size <c>50</c>.</p>
    
    
    <p>Arrange the nodes as described below.
            You can drag the nodes to place them manually, or for more precise placement,
            use "Anchor Presets".</p>
     </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-07-hud-topbar">
      <title>TopBar</title>
      <p>
        Theme Overrides, Constants
      <ol>
        <li>Margin Left = 20.</li>
        <li>Margin Right = 20.</li>
        <li>Margin Top = 20.</li>
        <li>Margin Bottom = 20.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      </subsection>
<subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-07-hud-scorelabel">
      <title>ScoreLabel</title>
      <p>
      <ol>
        <li>Add the text <c>0</c>.</li>
        <li>In "Container Sizing", "Horizontal", check the "Expand" box.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-07-hud-fuelgauge">
      <title>FuelGauge</title>
       <p>
      <ol>
        <li>Under "Range" set Max Value to <c>1.0</c> and Step to <c>0.05</c>.</li>
        <li><p>Under "Textures" for "Under, 
            <ol>
                <li>make a "NoiseTexture2D"</li> 
                <li>Width = <c>200</c> </li>
                <li> Height = <c>29</c></li>
                <li>Noise = "FastNoiseLite"</li>
                <li>Noise Type = "Cellular"</li>
                <li>Frequency = <c>0.3504</c></li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        </li>
        <li><p>Under "Textures" for "Over, 
            <ol>
                <li>load... "res://art/gauge_border.png"</li> 
            </ol>
        </p>
        </li>     
        <li><p>Under "Textures" for "Progress, 
            <ol>
                <li>make a "NoiseTexture2D"</li> 
                <li>Width = <c>200</c> </li>
                <li> Height = <c>27</c></li>
                <li>Noise = "FastNoiseLite"</li>
                <li>Noise Type = "Simplex"</li>
                <li>Frequency = <c>0.0015</c></li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        </li>
        <li><p>Under "Tint", 
            <ol>
                <li>Under: R:0.81, G:0.75, B:0.92, A:1.00</li> 
                <li>Over: R:0.86, G:0.73, B:0.91, A:1.00</li> 
                <li>Progress: R:0.72, G:0.45, B:0.24, A:1.00</li> 
            </ol>
        </p>
        </li>  
        <li>Under "Container Sizing" set "Horizontal" to Expand and set "Vertical" to "Shrink Center" </li>        
        </ol>
      </p>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-07-hud-healthbar">
      <title>Health</title>
      <p> To make Health, duplicate FuelGauge, rename the duplicate to "Health" and change the following
      <ol>
        
        <li><p>Under "Tint", 
            <ol>
                <li>Progress: R:0.18, G:0.44, B:0.24, A:1.00</li> 
            </ol>
        </p>
        </li>  
        <li>Under "Container Sizing" set "Horizontal"  Expand to be unchecked </li>        
        </ol>
      </p>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-07-hud-overlay">
      <title>Overlay</title>
      <p>
      <ol>
        <li>Set the "Anchors Preset" to <c>HCenter Wide</c>.</li>
        <li>Set the "Theme Overrides",  "Constants", "Separation" to <c>100</c>, otherwise the labels will be stacked.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      </subsection>
       <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-07-hud-message">
      <title>Message</title>
      <p>
      <ol>
        <li>Add the text <c>Space Farm</c>.</li>
        <li>Set the "Container Sizing", "Horizontal" to <c>Shrink Center</c>.</li>
        <li>Set the "Autowrap Mode" to <c>Word</c>, otherwise the label will stay on one line.</li>
        <li>Under "Control - Layout/Transform" set "Size X"  to <c>480</c> to use the entire width of the screen.</li>
        <li>Choose the "Anchor Preset" <c>Center</c>.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-07-hud-startbutton">
      <title>StartButton</title>
      <p>
      <ol>
        <li>Add the text <c>Start</c>.</li>
        <li>Under "Control - Theme Overrides - Constants", set "Outline Size" to <c>10</c> and "H Separatoin" to <c>10</c>
                        to add a little bit more padding between the border and text.</li>
        
        </ol>
      </p>
      <p>On the <c>MessageTimer</c>, set the <c>Wait Time</c> to <c>2</c> and set the <c>One Shot</c>
                property to "On".</p>
      <p>Now add this script to <c>HUD</c>:</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>extends CanvasLayer

# Notifies `Main` node that the button has been pressed
signal start_game</code>
          </program>


      <p>We now want to display a message temporarily,
                such as "Get Ready", so we add the following code</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>
func show_message(text: String, time:float = 2.0)  -> void:
    $Overlay/Message.text = text
    $Overlay/Message.show()
    if time > 0.0:
        $MessageTimer.start(time)
</code>
          </program>


      <p>We also need to process what happens when the player loses. The code below will show "Game Over" for 2 seconds, then return to the title screen and, after a brief pause, show the "Start" button.</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>
func game_over_sequence() -> void:
    show_message("Game Over")
    # Wait until the MessageTimer has counted down.
    await $MessageTimer.timeout

    show_message("Space Farm",0)
    # Make a one-shot timer and wait for it to finish.
    await get_tree().create_timer(1.0).timeout
    $Overlay/StartButton.show() 
</code>
          </program>


      
      <p>Add the code below to <c>HUD</c> to update the score</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>func update_score(score: int) -> void:
    $TopBar/HBoxContainer/ScoreLabel.text = str(score)
</code>
          </program>


      <p>Connect the <c>pressed()</c> signal of <c>StartButton</c> and the <c>timeout()</c>
                signal of <c>MessageTimer</c> to the <c>HUD</c> node, and add the following code to the new functions:</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>func _on_start_button_pressed() -> void:
    $StartButton.hide()
    start_game.emit()

func _on_message_timer_timeout() -> void:
    $Message.hide()</code>
          </program>


<p>Make <c>update_health()</c>  and  <c>update_fuel()</c>
                with the following code:</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>func update_health(health_ratio: float) -> void:
    $TopBar/HBoxContainer/Health.value=health_ratio

func update_fuel(fuel_ratio: float) -> void:
    $TopBar/HBoxContainer/FuelGauge.value=fuel_ratio
</code>
          </program>



      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-07-hud-connecting-hud-to-main">
      <title>Connecting HUD to Main</title>
      <p>Now that we're done creating the <c>HUD</c> scene, go back to <c>Main</c>. Instance
                the <c>HUD</c> scene in <c>Main</c> like you did the <c>Player</c> scene. The scene tree
                should look like this, so make sure you didn't miss anything:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Finished Main Scene</caption>
      <image source="completed_space_farm_main_scene.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>The HUD is the only additional node in the Main scene from the previous picture.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Now we need to connect the <c>HUD</c> functionality to our <c>Main</c> script. This
                requires a few additions to the <c>Main</c> scene:</p>
      <p>We start with adding a boolean to tell us if we are running, so that if we are not
        then we can stop (or not start) anything that happens while the game is running.
      </p>
      <program language="gdscript">
          <code>
            var game_on: bool = false
          </code>
        </program>
      <p>Next, We'll change ready to hide the player, update the view size, connect
        player wrecked to game_over, player health changed to the HUD's update health
        method, and the HUD's start_game signal to a new start_game method as shown below:
      </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>
func _ready() -> void:
    $Player.hide()
    update_view_size()
    $Player.wrecked.connect(game_over)
    $Player.health_changed.connect($HUD.update_health)
    $HUD.start_game.connect(start_game)
          </code>
          </program>


          <p>The game_over method in main will set game_on to false, then
            call the $HUD's game_over_sequence(), before the code that's already there</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>func game_over() -> void:
    game_on = false
    $HUD.game_over_sequence()
          </code>
        </program>
      <p>The start_game method needs to set game_on to true, reset position the player in the center, 
        show a message to get ready, reset the health value in the HUD, show the Player, and
        start the Plant timer.
      </p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>
func start_game() -> void:
    game_on = true
    $Player.position = view_size/2
    $HUD.show_message("Get Ready...",1.0)
    $HUD.update_health(1.0)
    $Player.show()
    $PlantTimer.start(1.0)
          </code>
          </program>


      <p>At the end of the <c>collect(amount: int)</c> method of main add an updated score to the score label of the HUD:</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>$HUD/TopBar/HBoxContainer/ScoreLabel.text = str(score)
    </code>
          </program>
          <p>In the <c>plan_crystal()</c> method of main add an if statment that checks to see if game_on is true before
        starting the PlantTimer. (make sure it's indented correctly)</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>if game_on:
    $PlantTimer.start(randf()*3+2)
    </code>
          </program>


      
      <p>Now you're ready to play! Click the "Play the Project" button.</p>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-07-hud-removing-old-creeps">
      <title>Play Testing</title>
      <p>If you play until "Game Over" and then start a new game, the prizes
                from the previous game will still be on the screen. The Player will still be in the same position,
                and it still won't be able to move. It would be better if the player could move and the crystals
                all disappeared at the start of a new game. 
      </p>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Resetting the Player</title>
            <p>
             In the player.gd script, in order to reset the player, we want to set it's position
             back to the center, set it's state to running, reset the health and fuel to max, and set can_launch to true.
             Since, the Player is a RigidBody2D, we can only reset the player location in the
             <c>_integrate_forces</c> method. So, we'll need to use an indicator for that part 
             of resetting the player. We'll call it <c>reset_pos</c>.
            </p>
            <p>At the top of the player.gd script add the <c>reset_pos</c> member variable.
            </p>
            <program language="gdscript">
          <code>
var reset_pos: bool = false
    </code>
          </program>
          <p>Then make a method called <c>_reset()</c> that will update everything described above
            except for the position.
            </p>
            <program language="gdscript">
          <code>
func _reset() -> void:
    change_state(State.RUNNING)
    health = max_health
    can_launch = true
    reset_pos = true
    </code>
          </program>
          <note>
            <title>health and fuel</title>
           <p>health is set to max_health instead of using a raw value (sometimes called a <term>magic number</term>), 
            and fuel is not in the main method </p>
            <p>
                If you haven't already, add a member variable called max_health, and set it to
                100, so that <c>_reset()</c> will work.
            </p>
            <p> 
                To connect fuel to the main method, you can do something similar to the way 
                health is connected to the HUD, but instead of taking damage, fuel should
                be lost when thrusting (and possibly when turning to a lesser extent).
                Fuel should be gained from collecting crystals. You may want the fuel to 
                increase slowly as the crystal is converted to fuel, or you may just want to
                make it so the fraction of the value of the crystal is used to increase fuel
                instantly.
            </p>
            
          </note>
          <p>Then add the following to <c>_integrate_forces()</c> so that the position will be 
            reset to the center. (Make sure it's indented correctly)
            </p>
            <program language="gdscript">
          <code>
if reset_pos:
        state.transform.origin = view_size/2
        reset_pos = false
    </code>
          </program>
      </paragraphs>
      
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Removing Prizes</title>
                <p>We  need a way to tell <em>all</em> the
                prizes to remove themselves. Recall from <xref ref="getting-started-first-2d-game-06-heads-up-display-removing-old-creeps"/> 
                that we can do this with the "group" feature.</p>
      <p>In the <c>Prize</c> scene, select the root node and click the <c>Groups</c> tab next to the
                <c>Signals</c> tab and the "+" button to open the "Create New Group" dialog.</p>
      
      <p>Name the group <c>prizes</c> and click "ok" to add a new scene group.
        Make sure to check the box next to the prizes group.
      Now all Prizes will be in the "prizes" group.</p>
      <p>Then add the following line to the <c>start_game()</c> function in <c>Main</c>:</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>get_tree().call_group("prizes", "queue_free")</code>
          </program>


      <p>The <c>call_group()</c> function calls the named function on every node in a
                group - in this case we are telling every prize to delete itself.</p>
        </paragraphs>
      <paragraphs>
  <title>Adding Levels</title>
      <p>
        After playing for a while, you may find that just waiting for crystals to grow,
         collecting crystals, and avoiding crystals can lack the element of surprize.
         Adding levels is one way to mix things up. You can base the levels on score, 
         counting how many of different size crystals were collected, etc.  
      </p>
      <p>
        The levels could increase in difficulty, or there could be different patterns of 
        how the crystals initially lay out. They could continue to spawn or you could just 
        need to clear the crystals that are initially placed at the beginning of the level.
      </p>
      <p> To keep track of levels you can use a member variable in main. Then you can 
        use <c>$HUD.show_message()</c> to indicate the level to the user at the beginning of
        each level.
      </p>
      <p>If you want specific patterns for each level, you may want to make a function that
        takes a level number and generates the locations of the initial crystals based on the 
        number. You probably want to modify plant_crystal to optionally take a position
        instead of randomly setting the position. In addition, you could have different spawning
        behavior based on the level. For instance, the spawning could put a crystal in an 
        initial position that was already collected.
      </p>
        
      </paragraphs>
      <p>
        Once the game plays as you want it with the current features, move on to the next
        section to create a more integrated interaction.
      </p>
  
      </subsection>
    
 
      
    </section>
